    {
      "title": "Weakening AI copyright law will spell ‘disaster for creativity’, global music body warns Albanese",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/09/weakening-ai-copyright-law-will-spell-disaster-for-creativity-australia",
      "description": "<p><strong>Exclusive:</strong> Head of IFPI says allowing AI companies access to online content would be a ‘huge mistake’ and damage Australia’s reputation</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Australia weakening copyright protections for AI companies would be “ridiculous and dangerous”, the global peak body for the music recording industry has warned as it watches with concern how the Albanese government responds to big tech’s demands.</p><p>While local creatives have <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/oct/31/experts-warn-ai-copyright-changes-could-breach-australian-constitution-and-spark-huge-compensation-rights-case\">raised constitutional issues</a> and <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/01/artists-against-ai-proposal-anthony-albanese-grayndler-sydney-inner-west\">threatened political backlash</a>, the International Federation of the Phonographic Industry (IFPI), representing recording industry bodies worldwide, said its members were worried about Australia potentially agreeing to demands other nations had rejected.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/09/weakening-ai-copyright-law-will-spell-disaster-for-creativity-australia\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-08T14:00:43"
    },
    {
      "title": "Cop31 must deliver climate progress that people can see is real, host says",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-climate-summit-deliver-progress-real-host-says",
      "description": "<p>High-stakes talks involving 190 countries begin on Monday as world grapples with El Niño supercharged by fossil fuels</p><p>The <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-what-is-at-stake-at-the-un-climate-summit-and-what-can-it-achieve\">Cop31 summit</a> must produce progress, “a tangible outcome ... that people can see is real”, its host has said as <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-key-players-climate-talks\">scores of world leaders and representatives of close to 190 countries</a> gather in Turkey for the UN climate talks.</p><p>The high-stakes meeting begins on Monday, as countries around the world grapple with the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/sep/21/super-el-nino-temperature-record\">strongest El Niño weather system on record</a>, supercharged by fossil fuels, which is set to take hundreds of thousands of lives and cause damage costing trillions of dollars.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-climate-summit-deliver-progress-real-host-says\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-08T13:24:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "Who’s who at Cop31: key players at the climate talks",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-key-players-climate-talks",
      "description": "<p>What are the relationships and dominant interests in play, as the summit kicks off after a bitter row over its hosting?</p><p>Hosting Cop was a <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/audio/2021/oct/25/australia-v-the-climate-part-1-kyoto\">key pledge from Australia’s prime minister, Anthony Albanese in 2021</a>, while in opposition, before his successful election campaign. But arcane rules meant <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/commentisfree/2022/sep/19/australia-is-pushing-to-host-a-cop-meeting-if-successful-it-would-be-forced-to-ramp-up-climate-action\">the country had to wait its turn to bid</a> and was then <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2025/jun/28/albanese-will-need-to-resolve-the-standoff-with-turkey-if-australia-is-to-host-cop31\">embroiled in an angry row with Turkey over the hosting</a>. The final compromise put Australia in charge of the negotiations which will take place in the Turkish city of Antalya. The bitter taste left by the row lingers and Australia has seemed to some a rather half-hearted host. The country’s media has been much quieter on Cop than previous host countries, and although Albanese will speak at the opening of the leaders’ segment, he is unlikely to stay long.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-key-players-climate-talks\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-08T11:48:56"
    },
    {
      "title": "Cop31: what is at stake at the UN climate summit and what can it achieve?",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-what-is-at-stake-at-the-un-climate-summit-and-what-can-it-achieve",
      "description": "<p>Delegates from more than 190 countries are gathering in Turkey for climate talks, but the US will be absent</p><p>As the planet faces unprecedented human-caused global heating, heads of government are meeting for the next fortnight for the latest round of climate talks. We ask why it matters and what is likely to be achieved.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-what-is-at-stake-at-the-un-climate-summit-and-what-can-it-achieve\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-08T11:00:38"
    },
    {
      "title": "Psychiatrists and paediatricians among specialists most likely to charge ‘extreme’ fees, thinktank finds",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/08/psychiatrists-paediatricians-extreme-specialist-fees-health-australia",
      "description": "<p>Grattan Institute finds some doctors charge up to triple the fees of their peers – well beyond what can be justified by business running costs</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>More than 2.2 million Australians paid an “extreme” fee to see a specialist last year, with psychiatrists and paediatricians among the most likely to routinely unreasonably charge their patients, according to new research.</p><p>Specialists cannot justify exorbitant fees by blaming inadequate government rebates, or the costs of running a business, a report from policy thinktank the Grattan Institute found, as these factors explain only a fraction of the fee increases Australians have faced.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/08/psychiatrists-paediatricians-extreme-specialist-fees-health-australia\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-08T09:00:35"
    },
    {
      "title": "A Guide to COP31 Climate Talks in Antalya, Turkey",
      "link": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/08/climate/what-is-cop31-climate.html",
      "description": "Negotiators and leaders are gathering for annual United Nations climate talks. They come as global temperatures are set to breach a key threshold.",
      "source_name": "NYT World News",
      "published": "2026-11-08T10:01:50"
    }
